# Every layer any document declares, whatever it went on to say about it. One row per
# (filing, layer): the roster, before any of the vectors decide whether they have an entry for it.
#
# Every other relation here filters this one
#   Every other relation in this directory is a filter on this one, and this is where the thing
#   being filtered has its name. `layers/demand.sqlc` keeps the layers with a stated demand,
#   `layers/nameplate.sqlc` those with a stated amount, `layers/absorber.sqlc` those whose
#   taxonomy a reader has mapped. Each is correct, and each is a sparse reading of one population;
#   without a name for that population, a caller needing existence alone reaches into `pm.layer`
#   raw, projecting no column of it, because there is nothing to compose.
#
# Existence is not a column
#   That is why this file looks empty. It carries the key and nothing else on purpose. A caller
#   wanting a layer's demand composes layers/demand.sqlc; a caller asking only whether the layer
#   is composes this. `checks/local_part_dangles.sqlc` asks the complement, left-joining this
#   relation to find the local parts that name no layer.
#
# A denominator, never a membership test
#   The positive question, whether one named layer exists, is asked of `pm.layer` directly, as
#   `composition/parts.sqlc` does. Inner-joining this relation for a membership test would hand the
#   whole dimension to every consumer downstream; `algebra/dimension_use.sqlc` states that rule and
#   holds every parent that inner-joins this file to a declared exception.
#
# The denominator for every coverage figure
#   Run it alone and it is the denominator for every coverage figure in the repository. Any "so
#   many of the lumpy layers" needs its denominator from somewhere, and this is where.
-- pm:Stack/pm:layer, keyed and nothing more.
SELECT l.filing, l.layer
FROM pm.layer l
